  "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$",
